===Etymology 2===
{{inh+|en|enm|goode||good, well|pos=adverb}}, from the adjective. Compare {{cog|nl|goed||good, well|pos=adverb}}, {{cog|de|gut||good, well|pos=adverb}}, {{cog|da|godt||good, well|pos=adverb}}, {{cog|sv|gott||good, well|pos=adverb}}, all from the adjective.

====Adverb====
{{en-adv|better|sup=best}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} [[well|Well]]; satisfactorily or thoroughly.
#: {{ux|en|When you're cleaning these racks, you've got to get in there '''good''', because the quality system specifies it.}}
#: {{ux|en|The boy done '''good'''.|q=did well}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1906|author=Zane Grey|title=The Spirit of the Border: A Romance of the Early Settlers in the Ohio Valley|passage=If Silvertip refuses to give you the horse, grab him before he can draw a weapon, and beat him '''good'''. You're big enough to do it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Troy Conway|title=The Cunning Linguist|location=London|publisher=Flamingo Books|year=1970|page=66|passage=I kept my eyes peeled for signs of pursuit. There was none, unless I was being fooled very '''good'''.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1972|author=w:Harry Chapin|album=Sniper and Other Love Songs|title=A Better Place to Be|passage=She said, "I don't want to bother you / Consider it's understood / I know I'm not no beauty queen / But I sure can listen '''good'''."}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1994|title=w:Pulp Fiction|writer=Quentin Tarantino; Roger Avary|role=Jules|actor=Samuel L. Jackson|passage=Marsellus fucked him up '''good'''. Word 'round the campfire is it was on account of Marsellus Wallace's wife.}}
#* '''2007''' April 19, [[w:Jimmy Wales|Jimmy Wales]], “Jimmy Wales on the User-Generated Generation”, ''[[w:Fresh Air|Fresh Air]]'', WHYY, Pennsylvania [https://web.archive.org/web/20070423020137/http://www.npr.org/templates/story/story.php?storyId=9683874]
#*: The one thing that we can't do''...''is throw out the baby with the bathwater.''...'' We know our process works pretty darn '''good''' and, uh, it’s really sparked this amazing phenomenon of this''...''high-quality website.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=2010|author=w:Monte Dwyer|title=Red in the Centre: Through a Crooked Lens|publisher=Monyer Pty Ltd|page=14|passage="They're travellin' '''good''' now. We'll leave them be."}}
#* {{quote-video game|en|developer=w:BioWare|title=w:Mass Effect 3|genre=[[science fiction|Science Fiction]]|location=Redwood City|publisher=Electronic Arts|year=2012|system=PC|scene={{w|Citadel (Mass Effect)|Citadel}} (Priority: Earth)|oclc=962368035|passage={{w|David Anderson (Mass Effect)|Admiral Anderson}}: You did '''good''', child. You did '''good'''. I'm proud of you.}}

=====Usage notes=====
The Germanic cognates (listed in the [[#Etymology_2|Etymology]]) illustrate a [[null morpheme]] difference from the adjective that is fully {{glossary|standard}} in those languages, as it would be in modern English [[if not for]] a widespread {{glossary|prescriptive}} misapprehension. The idea that adverbs in English cannot or should not have a [[null morpheme]] difference from the adjective is a widespread notion that bars ''good'' ([[adverb#Noun|adv]]) and ''[[bad#Adverb|bad]]'' (adv) from {{glossary|formal}} use even though they are [[ubiquitous]] in {{glossary|informal}} use. It also sometimes drives people to inject ''[[-ly#Suffix|-ly]]'' [[hypercorrection|hypercorrectively]]; for example, ''[[thusly]]'' is stated by most usage guides to be a needless replacement of ''[[thus#Adverb|thus]]''.

